    <item>
      <title>Ford unveils the GT41 as it hopes to win Le Mans again</title>
      <author>
                    Jonathan M. Gitlin
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/cars/2026/11/ford-unveils-the-gt41-as-it-hopes-to-win-le-mans-again/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/cars/2026/11/ford-unveils-the-gt41-as-it-hopes-to-win-le-mans-again/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">6dfc4135e88863b40d96533fa2653e02b7419cb2</guid>
      <pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 18:00:29 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;       Ford enters the World Endurance Championship in 2027 with the new prototype.     &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="3000" height="2000" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41-and-GT40_06.jpg" class="intro-image" alt="A render of the Ford GT41 next to the GT40" loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41-and-GT40_06.jpg 3000w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41-and-GT40_06-640x427.jpg 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41-and-GT40_06-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41-and-GT40_06-768x512.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41-and-GT40_06-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41-and-GT40_06-2048x1365.jpg 2048w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41-and-GT40_06-980x653.jpg 980w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41-and-GT40_06-1440x960.jpg 1440w" sizes="(max-width: 3000px) 100vw, 3000px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       The GT40 (right) won Le Mans 1966-1969; now Ford wants to add some more.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                       Ford                    &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;Sixty years ago, after two failed attempts, Ford won the 24 Hours of Le Mans with its GT40 race car. In the years since, the Blue Oval has been back to France more than a few times, with varying success. It could never replicate the GT40’s success winning overall, but it celebrated the 40th anniversary &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/cars/2016/07/after-victory-at-le-mans-we-catch-up-with-ford-to-talk-about-its-gt/"&gt;with a class win&lt;/a&gt;. Now it wants another shot at the &lt;a href="https://www.24h-lemans.com/en/news/how-are-24-hours-of-le-mans-trophies-made-56324"&gt;really big trophy&lt;/a&gt;, and it debuted the car during the Detroit Lions-Chicago Bears football game today: meet the GT41.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“Revealing the Ford GT41 is a significant moment for everyone involved in this program,” said Dan Sayers, manager of Ford Racing’s Hypercar program. “It’s the result of an enormous amount of work across our engineering, design, and development teams and an important milestone on our journey towards 2027. We know the scale of the challenge ahead—the level of competition in Hypercar is incredibly high, and there are no shortcuts to success. Our focus has been on building the right foundations, bringing together the right people and making sure we learn as much as possible from every stage of development.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;figure class="ars-video ars-video--horizontal"&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;div class="relative"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/embed/0gyCYvhtHSI?start=0&amp;amp;wmode=transparent"&gt;https://www.youtube.com/embed/0gyCYvhtHSI?start=0&amp;amp;wmode=transparent&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="caption font-impact dusk:text-gray-300 mb-4 mt-2 inline-flex flex-row items-stretch gap-1 text-base leading-tight text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-300"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-icon bg-[left_top_5px] w-[10px] shrink-0"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-content"&gt;       The video was directed by Zack Snyder and features Matt Damon’s voice-over.            &lt;/div&gt;   &lt;/div&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/figure&gt; &lt;p&gt;Like many other entrants in the World Endurance Championship’s Hypercar class, Ford has chosen the rear-wheel-drive LMDh technical approach rather than building the GT41 entirely from scratch, as Ferrari and Aston Martin do with their cars. LMDh cars start with one of four approved carbon fiber chassis—in this case, Ford is working with Oreca, which has also partnered with Genesis and Acura.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;The hybrid system and transmission are also standard parts. But Ford supplies the engine—a 5.4 L V8 based on the Mustang’s Coyote engine—as well as software to control the hybrid powertrain. A non-hybridized version of the Coyote has already been racing effectively under the hood of the Ford Mustang GT3.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;div class="ars-lightbox align-fullwidth my-5"&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img decoding="async" width="1024" height="683" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_12-1024x683.jpg" class="ars-gallery-image" alt="" loading="lazy" aria-labelledby="caption-2183864" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_12-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_12-640x427.jpg 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_12-768x512.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_12-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_12-2048x1365.jpg 2048w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_12-980x653.jpg 980w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_12-1440x960.jpg 1440w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       The testing livery has easter eggs in it.               &lt;div class="ars-gallery-caption-credit"&gt;Credit:                        Ford                   &lt;/div&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img decoding="async" width="1024" height="683" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_14-1024x683.jpg" class="ars-gallery-image" alt="Ford GT41 details" loading="lazy" aria-labelledby="caption-2183863" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_14-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_14-640x427.jpg 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_14-768x512.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_14-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_14-2048x1365.jpg 2048w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_14-980x653.jpg 980w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Ford-GT41_14-1440x960.jpg 1440w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       Spot the road car styling cues.           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The technical regulations also allow for more styling freedom than we often see in racing. The absolute amount of aerodynamic downforce is capped, as is the minimum drag, so OEMs are encouraged to use road car styling details to create a closer link between prototype and road car. I’ll probably need to spend more time looking at it to spot those road car cues, but I do see quite a few superficial similarities with the Genesis LMDh car, as well as the &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/cars/2025/09/behind-the-scenes-with-the-most-beautiful-car-in-racing-the-ferrari-499p/"&gt;Ferrari Hypercar&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Despite being an American car brand, Ford doesn’t have plans as yet to enter IMSA’s WeatherTech championship, so don’t expect to see the GT41 next January racing at Daytona. Instead, it will take its first green flag in Qatar in March, the first of WEC’s nine races next season.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Ford should find it an extremely competitive championship. Although Peugeot and Alpine will not race next year, McLaren is also entering, alongside returning teams from Aston Martin, BMW, Cadillac, Genesis, Ferrari, and Toyota, which this year won Le Mans and the overall title, following three years of Ferrari domination.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>24-hours-of-le-mans</category>
      <category>ford-gt41</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Draining peatlands is responsible for a few percent of our global warming</title>
      <author>
                    Scott K. Johnson
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/11/draining-peatlands-is-responsible-for-a-few-percent-of-our-global-warming/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/11/draining-peatlands-is-responsible-for-a-few-percent-of-our-global-warming/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">8e12b5cefe9dc3ce0ece981588ad071d4ca8503c</guid>
      <pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 12:15:42 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Detailed estimate going back to 1800 shows how much carbon is being liberated.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1296653735-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="overhead view of the border between an orderly planted array of crop trees and a chaotic jungle canopy." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1296653735-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1296653735-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1296653735-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1296653735-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;                       &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                        &lt;a class="caption-credit-link text-gray-400 no-underline hover:text-gray-500" href="https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/sustainable-aerial-view-palm-oil-plantation-with-royalty-free-image/1296653735?phrase=peatland"               target="_blank"&gt;                      Muslianshah Masrie                        &lt;/a&gt;                   &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;Much of the focus for human-caused climate change is on fossil fuels, which are creating most of our problems. But land use change is also a large component of the story, accounting for about one-third of total historical CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; emissions. Deforestation plays a major role here, but practices like draining wetlands also factor in. Exactly how much of our history of eliminating wetlands has contributed to warming is challenging to figure out. But thanks to new sources of data that measure emissions from today’s surviving wetlands, it’s possible to put a better number on it.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;A new study led by Jevan Yu at MIT focuses on peatlands—environments like bogs and fens where organic matter accumulates into thick layers of material. Some 600 billion tons of carbon are held in these vast stores of peat, which is more than you can find in all the world’s living vegetation, the researchers note.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Because these areas are permanently inundated, any oxygen in the water is quickly used up, leaving the job of decaying all that organic matter to anaerobic bacteria that work much more slowly and don’t digest everything. So instead of seeing organic carbon turning into the CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; gas released by aerobic bacteria, in peatlands, a subset of it gradually turns into methane gas. Methane is an even more potent greenhouse gas than CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; itself and ultimately gets oxidized into CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;. That means there’s a little trade-off from peatlands—most of the carbon they have is kept out of the atmosphere, but the carbon that does escape does so as methane.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Many peatlands have been drained by digging ditches that let water flow out to repurpose the land for agriculture or other uses. That lowers the water level, supplying oxygen to the exposed peat and ringing the aerobic bacteria dinner bell. Methane emissions get turned down, and CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; emissions get turned way up.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;History re: peats&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;The researchers used recent studies that measure gas emissions from peatlands to build a global model that estimated CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and methane release as the water level drops. The two gases respond differently to a given drop in water level, and CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; in particular responds differently depending on where in the world the bog is located. Using reconstructions of water-level changes due to historical peatland drainage, the team ran their model starting in 1800.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;The simplest way to represent the climate impact of both CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and methane changes was to calculate the change in Earth’s energy budget—the strength of the greenhouse effect, in other words. They calculated the total effect in 2020 at about +83 milliwatts per square meter of the Earth’s surface from the CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; emissions, and about -9 milliwatts per square meter from the reduced methane emissions, for a total effect of +73 mW. That is 2.7 percent of the total human-caused change in Earth’s energy budget. (With the error bars on all these numbers, it’s 1.8–3.6 percent.)&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Or to look at it another way, it means that around 4 percent of all the carbon that was buried in peatlands is now in the atmosphere.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Regionally, over half of the total CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; came from Europe, almost a quarter came from Southeast Asia, and only 14 percent came from North America. The timing is quite different, as well, with the contribution from Southeast Asia only jumping up in the last few decades.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;div class="ars-lightbox"&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1024" height="732" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/peatland_drainage_climate_impact_fig4-1024x732.png" class="none large" alt="Image of four charts." decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/peatland_drainage_climate_impact_fig4-1024x732.png 1024w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/peatland_drainage_climate_impact_fig4-640x458.png 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/peatland_drainage_climate_impact_fig4-768x549.png 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/peatland_drainage_climate_impact_fig4-1536x1098.png 1536w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/peatland_drainage_climate_impact_fig4-980x701.png 980w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/peatland_drainage_climate_impact_fig4-1440x1030.png 1440w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/peatland_drainage_climate_impact_fig4.png 1906w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;               Regional emissions timeline (left) and total shares (right) for carbon dioxide (top) and methane (bottom).                               &lt;div class="ars-gallery-caption-credit"&gt;Credit:                    Credit:                                       Yu, et al./Nature                                   &lt;/div&gt;                           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Draining a peatland only &lt;em&gt;starts&lt;/em&gt; the process of releasing carbon, though. So that means past land-use change will still be producing emissions in the future. To examine this, the researchers also ran their model forward to 2100.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;If peatland water levels were simply held constant from 2020 on, the total change in Earth’s energy budget would nearly double, from 73 to 138 milliwatts per square meter by 2100. Moderate restoration of peatland water levels could reduce that growth, and ambitious restoration could even cause the effect to shrink slightly below its 2020 potency in their analysis. This is a process we can halt if we really want to.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But since rolling all the way back to the landscape of 1800 is unlikely, elevated emissions will continue for a long time. That makes this another part of the climate system where change plays out slowly, with long-term consequences.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Nature, 2026. DOI: &lt;a href="http://dx.doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-11339-0"&gt;10.1038/s41586-026-11339-0&lt;/a&gt; (&lt;a href="http://arstechnica.com/science/news/2010/03/dois-and-their-discontents-1.ars"&gt;About DOIs&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;</description>
