      <title>Backblaze explains why it's sharing customer information with advertisers</title>
      <author>
                    Scharon Harding
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2026/11/backblaze-explains-why-its-sharing-customer-information-with-advertisers/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2026/11/backblaze-explains-why-its-sharing-customer-information-with-advertisers/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">26689bb8215bae3c7a115962d199cf5b5a964225</guid>
      <pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 18:38:29 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Backblaze tells Ars it won&amp;#8217;t share users&amp;#8217; backup, storage files with advertisers.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1219679299-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="Close-up of the hands of two suited men exchanging a $100 bill." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1219679299-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1219679299-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1219679299-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/GettyImages-1219679299-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;                       &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                        &lt;a class="caption-credit-link text-gray-400 no-underline hover:text-gray-500" href="https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/employees-are-doing-corruption-by-giving-dirty-royalty-free-image/1219679299"               target="_blank"&gt;                      Getty                        &lt;/a&gt;                   &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;Some customers have raised concerns about Backblaze’s privacy policy taking effect next month, which says the backup and cloud storage company may sell certain personal information to advertisers.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Backblaze’s &lt;a href="https://www.backblaze.com/company/policy/privacy-april-2026"&gt;current privacy policy&lt;/a&gt; says that in addition to the personal information that users may provide, such as their email address when creating an account, it may also “automatically” collect information, such as “your software, the operating system you use when accessing our service, your Internet Protocol address, and the date and time of each request you make to Backblaze.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The current policy adds: “Under no circumstances do we rent, trade, or share your address or e-mail address with any other company for their marketing purposes without your consent.” It also makes no mention of advertisers.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;On December 1, a &lt;a href="https://www.backblaze.com/company/policy/privacy"&gt;new privacy policy&lt;/a&gt; takes effect. It includes a table listing the types of “general personal information” that Backblaze says it has been collecting and sharing with third parties, including “advertising and marketing partners” since December 1, 2025, This information includes identifiers (such as “name, address, Internet Protocol address, [and] email address”); commercial information (such as “records of personal property, products or services purchased, obtained, or considered, or other purchasing or consuming histories or tendencies”; geolocation data that “can be used to determine a device’s physical location”; and “sensory or surveillance data” (which includes “call recordings or other audio, electronic, or visual monitoring or surveillance”).&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This has &lt;a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/backblaze/comments/1y1mx2c/backblazes_dec_1_policy_update_is_more_than_a/"&gt;concerned some users,&lt;/a&gt; but a Backblaze spokesperson told Ars Technica that “Backblaze does not sell or share sensory or surveillance data, such as call recordings, for targeted advertising, and we do not disclose it to advertising or marketing partners.” They added:&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;blockquote&gt; &lt;div&gt;The Privacy Policy table lists one set of recipients across all data categories, which is why it can read differently. Our legal team will review the table for clarity as part of our next review cycle.&lt;/div&gt; &lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;h2&gt;No “sensitive personal information” shared&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;Notably, no “sensitive personal information,” such as social security numbers, credit card numbers, or “precise geolocation,” is shared with advertisers, the policy states. However, identifiers, commercial information, “Internet or Other Similar Network Activity,” and “geolocation data” may be “sold or shared for targeted Advertising”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Critically, the upcoming policy “only applies to the information collected in respect of your account and not to the content that you store on our system.” Backblaze’s broader terms of service also &lt;a href="https://www.backblaze.com/company/policy/terms-of-service"&gt;state that&lt;/a&gt;: “Files stored using Backblaze Computer Backup are encrypted prior to being sent to us.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;However, &lt;a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/backblaze/comments/1y1mx2c/backblazes_dec_1_policy_update_is_more_than_a/"&gt;some customers remain concerned&lt;/a&gt; that the privacy policy doesn’t more clearly and distinctly explain that Backblaze won’t share or sell content from customers’ backup files. In response, Backblaze’s representative explained:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is because the Privacy Notice’s sale and sharing disclosures relate to website and marketing data, such as information collected through cookies and similar technologies on our site. They do not apply to files customers store with us.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;Renatta Siewert, Backblaze’s director of communications and brand programs, reiterated to Ars that Backblaze doesn’t share the contents of backup or storage files with advertisers:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;Backblaze takes the privacy and security of our customers’ data seriously. We updated our Privacy Notice to keep pace with the changing legal landscape, which increasingly requires companies to spell out how they collect and use marketing-related information. This update gives customers a clearer, more specific view of what we collect and why. These disclosures are separate from the files customers entrust to Backblaze for backup and storage, which we do not sell or share with advertisers, data brokers, or analytics partners.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;Customers can &lt;a href="https://preferences.backblaze.com/form/opt_out"&gt;use this link&lt;/a&gt; to opt out of the selling or sharing of their personal information for cross-context behavioral advertising.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
